@page "/mcp-server"
@using Sedna.UI.Catalogue.Navigation

@* Documents the endpoint this same application serves. The route is /mcp-server
   and not /mcp, because Program.cs maps the MCP transport itself at /mcp. *@

<CatHead Title="MCP server" Tier="@CatHead.Site">
    This site answers to agents as well as to people. One read-only endpoint, its tools and four
    resources, over the same bytes the pages render.
</CatHead>

<div class="callout callout--info">
    <i class="ri-shield-check-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">Public, unauthenticated and read-only</span>
        Every tool is annotated
        <code>readOnlyHint</code>, so a client that honours the hint calls them without prompting, and
        <strong>there will never be a tool that writes</strong> — enforced by a test.
    </div>
</div>

<h2 id="add-it">Add it</h2>
<p class="cat-lede">One URL. Streamable HTTP, no key, no install.</p>

<CatSnippet Name="Mcp/Config.txt" Title="Any MCP client">
    <p>
        The endpoint is <code>@CatalogueLinks.Mcp</code>. It is stateless, so a client may reconnect
        or fan out across processes without leaving sessions behind.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<CatSnippet Name="Mcp/ClaudeCode.txt" Title="Claude Code">
    <p>Or add it to a project's <code>.mcp.json</code> with the block above.</p>
</CatSnippet>

<h2 id="tools">The tools</h2>
<p class="cat-lede">
    One verb each, no overlap, shaped around one workflow: an agent writing a page in a consuming app
    and needing the exact markup plus enough semantics to pick the right variant &mdash; then checking
    what it wrote, and upgrading the app later.
</p>

<div class="card sedna-mb-3">
    <table class="table">
        <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it answers</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><code>search</code></td>
                <td>
                    “What is there for a sortable table with status badges?” Ranks examples,
                    classes, tokens and pages together. <strong>Returns references, never
                    markup</strong>.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>get_example</code></td>
                <td>
                    The markup for up to five examples, byte-for-byte what this site renders. Valid
                    in a <code>.razor</code> page and an <code>.html</code> file alike.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>describe_class</code></td>
                <td>
                    What a class actually does: the declarations from the shipped stylesheet, its
                    cascade layer, its modifiers, its family's variants, the attributes and
                    pseudo-classes that put it in a state, and which examples use it. Also the way to check a
                    class your app already styles before upgrading; a class a release renamed comes
                    back with the classes that replace it.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>get_page</code></td>
                <td>Every example on one page — one call instead of five. With no id, the page list.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>get_tokens</code></td>
                <td>
                    The tokens as an ordered array of blocks, media condition included, for writing
                    <code>brand.css</code>.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>get_integration_guide</code></td>
                <td>
                    The host page and its load order, the branding recipe, the JavaScript and C#
                    surface, the rules a consuming app follows, the graph's or the rich-text
                    editor's reference, or &mdash; with <code>"upgrade"</code> &mdash; every class
                    each release renamed, old name to the classes that look the same.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>check_markup</code></td>
                <td>
                    For markup the agent wrote: every class the stylesheet does not declare, with the
                    nearest library class in case it is a typo; every class a release renamed or
                    removed, judged against the installed version; every class newer than it; and
                    every icon the font does not have. Classes only &mdash; it does not judge structure.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>get_changes</code></td>
                <td>
                    What two releases differ by: every class, token and public C# member added and
                    removed, every rename, and each release in between with a link to its notes,
                    where the breaking changes are listed first. <code>to: "main"</code> includes
                    what is merged and in no release yet.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>describe_api</code></td>
                <td>
                    The C# and script API, member by member: a type's members, a member's signatures,
                    its documentation, the release it first shipped in and the examples that use it
                    &mdash; <code>ISednaUi.ShowModalAsync</code>, or <code>sednaUi.toast</code>.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<CatSnippet Name="Mcp/SearchCall.txt" Title="The whole loop">
    <p>
        <code>search</code> → <code>get_example</code> → <code>describe_class</code>. Nothing else
        is needed to write a page; <code>check_markup</code> then checks it.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<CatSnippet Name="Mcp/DescribeClassCall.txt" Title="`describe_class`">
    <p>
        Up to ten class names per call. The declarations come from the stylesheet the running app
        serves, and <code>usedByExamples</code> points at the pages that show the class in context.
        <code>variants</code> and <code>states</code> are derived from the selectors: a state is an
        attribute or a pseudo-class written on the class itself, never one on an ancestor.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<h2 id="resources">The four resources</h2>
<p class="cat-lede">
    Whole artefacts, addressable by URI. A resource is attached by the person rather than pulled by
    the model. Nothing is resource-only: <code>get_tokens</code> and
    <code>get_integration_guide</code> duplicate two of these, because clients vary widely in
    resource support.
</p>

<div class="card sedna-mb-3">
    <table class="table">
        <thead><tr><th>URI</th><th>Type</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><code>sednaui://stylesheet</code></td>
                <td class="col-muted"><code>text/css</code></td>
                <td>The whole shipped stylesheet, exactly as the package delivers it.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>sednaui://tokens</code></td>
                <td class="col-muted"><code>application/json</code></td>
                <td>The token export, as an ordered array of blocks.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>sednaui://version</code></td>
                <td class="col-muted"><code>application/json</code></td>
                <td>What this catalogue was built from, and the latest released version.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>sednaui://docs/{name}</code></td>
                <td class="col-muted"><code>text/markdown</code></td>
                <td>
                    Any file in the repository's <code>docs/</code>, by name without
                    <code>.md</code> &mdash; <code>getting-started</code>, <code>architecture</code>,
                    <code>CLAUDE.consuming-app</code> and the rest. A wrong name is answered with the list.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<h2 id="versions">Say which version you have</h2>
<p class="cat-lede">
    This site is built from <code>main</code> and can be ahead of any release, so every tool that
    answers with classes, tokens, examples or API takes <code>installedVersion</code>. Pass it and the <code>meta.warning</code> block names everything
    in the answer your version does not have — including anything with no <code>since</code> at all,
    which is in <em>no</em> release yet. The results still come back.
</p>

<h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
<p class="cat-lede">
    They apply to <code>/mcp</code> alone, never globally — a global limiter would also count this
    site's own SignalR upgrades. A rejected call answers <code>429</code> with
    <code>Retry-After: 60</code>.
</p>

<div class="card sedna-mb-3">
    <table class="table">
        <thead><tr><th>Limit</th><th>Value</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Concurrent requests</td><td class="col-num">64, plus 32 queued</td>
                <td>The actual control. Not partitioned, so there is nothing to spoof around.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Per caller</td><td class="col-num">240 burst, 120/min</td>
                <td>
                    Fairness, not security: behind a proxy whose addresses we do not control, per-IP
                    limiting is not a security measure.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Request body</td><td class="col-num">256 KB</td>
                <td>JSON-RPC calls here are a few hundred bytes.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Per call</td>
                <td class="col-num">25 / 5 / 10</td>
                <td>
                    Hits from <code>search</code>, ids per <code>get_example</code>, names per
                    <code>describe_class</code>. Markup is capped at 8&nbsp;KB per example and cut
                    at a tag boundary, so a truncated response still parses. <code>describe_api</code>
                    takes 10 names, and <code>check_markup</code> up to 64&nbsp;KB of markup.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<h2 id="agent-rules">Then give the agent the rules</h2>
<p class="cat-lede">
    The server answers questions. It does not stop an agent inventing a class name or wrapping a
    table in a component — so copy
    <CatLink Href="https://github.com/dennisrahmen/Sedna.UI/blob/main/docs/CLAUDE.consuming-app.md">
    <code>docs/CLAUDE.consuming-app.md</code></CatLink> into your app's own
    <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, or fetch it through
    <code>get_integration_guide { "section": "rules" }</code>.
</p>
